Claim: acts of terrorism that he believes anyone making AGI must do

## Your earlier research
The claim mischaracterizes Yudkowsky's 'pivotal act' concept. From my background knowledge (I did not run a search), Eliezer Yudkowsky and MIRI define a pivotal act as an action by an AGI-enabled actor that substantially reduces the risk of existential catastrophe from other AGIs. He has said such acts need not be violent. His standard example is using an aligned AGI to 'burn all GPUs' (more precisely, to melt them with nanotechnology). That example is a drastic, unilateral and arguably illegal act, so critics do call it coercive or terrorism-like. But Yudkowsky does not say that 'anyone making AGI must' do such acts. He describes a pivotal act as something a careful, aligned-AGI-wielding group might need to do. He has also said that proposals like 'make the AGI a nice democratic world government' are not workable, and he has stressed that the act must prevent unaligned AGI from destroying the world. In his April 2023 TIME op-ed he advocated international treaties and, if necessary, airstrikes on rogue datacenters. People widely criticized this as endorsing violence, but it is state enforcement and not a requirement on AGI makers. The claim is an opinion framed as belief ('I believe'). It overgeneralizes by saying 'must' and by calling the concept a euphemism for terrorism. Reasonable people can argue the burn-GPUs example amounts to a coercive unilateral act, but the 'terrorism' and 'anyone making AGI must' framing is misleading. No source URL was retrieved. Relevant background is on LessWrong/Arbital under 'pivotal act' (https://arbital.com/p/pivotal/).

